<p class="lead">To calculate paint for a vaulted ceiling, add the sloped surface areas, multiply by coats and your chosen allowance, then divide by label coverage. A 400 sq ft ceiling needs 2.2 US gallons at 400 sq ft per US gallon, two coats and 10% extra, before rounding to whole cans.</p>
<h2>What measurements do you need for a sloped ceiling?</h2>
<p>Sketch each ceiling face separately. For a simple two-sided vault with a straight ridge, each face is a sloping rectangle: its area is the length along the ridge multiplied by its sloped width from the wall to the ridge. Split irregular flat faces into rectangles and triangles measured in the ceiling plane before adding their areas.</p>
<p>Use dimensions from suitable plans or safe professional measurements for high, inaccessible surfaces. Write down:</p>
<ol>
<li>The length of each ceiling plane along the ridge.</li>
<li>The sloped width of each plane, if already known.</li>
<li>Otherwise, the horizontal run and vertical rise for each side.</li>
<li>Any flat ceiling strip, large opening or separate painted face.</li>
</ol>
<p>The horizontal run is the level distance from one side wall to the point directly beneath the ridge. The rise is the vertical difference between that wall's ceiling edge and the ridge, not the ridge height above the floor.</p>
<p>When those distances form a right triangle, sloped width = √(run² + rise²), using the <a href="https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/c-geometric-formulas">Pythagorean theorem and rectangle-area formulas</a>. An 8 ft run and 6 ft rise give a 10 ft slope. For the rest of the room, keep a separate <a href="/guides/measure-room-for-paint/">room-measurement worksheet</a>.</p>
<h2>How do you calculate a symmetrical vaulted ceiling?</h2>
<p>Suppose the ceiling is 20 ft long, spans 16 ft between equal-height wall edges and rises 6 ft to a centred ridge. In metric, those dimensions are 6.096 m, 4.8768 m and 1.8288 m.</p>
<p>Each horizontal run is half the span: 8 ft. Each slope is √(8² + 6²) = 10 ft, or 3.048 m. Multiply the slope by the ridge length for each plane, then add them.</p>
