<p>The displayed measurements are rounded; keep full precision through the calculation. If the wall edges sit at different heights, each side needs its own vertical rise as well as its own horizontal run. A flat strip or another ceiling plane gets its own worksheet row.</p>
<h2>How do area, coats and label coverage become cans?</h2>
<p>Use this purchasing formula for each compatible paint product, after subtracting unpainted openings from the measured surface area:</p>
<p>Paint volume = painted area × finish coats × (1 + allowance percentage ÷ 100) ÷ one-coat label coverage.</p>
<p>Whole cans = round up (paint volume ÷ volume per can).</p>
<p>Keep the units paired: sq ft with sq ft per US gallon, or m² with m² per litre. Use your actual product label and substrate conditions rather than treating a worksheet rate as universal; <a href="https://www.sherwin-williams.com/en-us/color/color-tools/paint-calculator">Sherwin-Williams identifies application method and surface condition as quantity variables</a>.</p>
<p>A product-specific reference is <a href="https://www.benjaminmoore.com/en-us/c/ceiling-paint">Benjamin Moore Waterborne Ceiling Paint's published 400-450 sq ft per gallon</a>, with one or two coats recommended. For the symmetrical ceiling, use the lower 400 sq ft rate and a chosen 10% planning allowance.</p>
