<p>For the first row, 400 × 2 × 1.10 ÷ 400 = 2.20 US gallons. For the second, 35.1246118 × 2 × 1.10 ÷ 10 = 7.7274 L. Round only after combining compatible planes, not after each side.</p>
<p>Choose the coat count with the product instructions and the <a href="/guides/paint-coats/">one-coat versus two-coat guide</a>. Calculate primer separately with its own coverage and coats. For other container sizes, the <a href="/guides/paint-coverage/">paint-coverage guide</a> explains the unit conversions.</p>
<h2>Do gable walls and skylights belong in the ceiling total?</h2>
<p>A triangular gable wall is vertical wall area, not another sloping ceiling plane. Its area is half its base multiplied by its vertical height, following the <a href="https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/c-geometric-formulas">triangle-area formula</a>.</p>
<p>In the symmetrical example, one end-wall triangle is 16 × 6 ÷ 2 = 48 sq ft. Two matching ends add 96 sq ft, or 8.92 m², to the wall worksheet, not to the 400 sq ft ceiling total.</p>
<p>Deduct a skylight's actual unpainted opening area in the sloping ceiling plane, not its horizontal floor projection. Count any painted recess faces separately. Keep walls, trim or recesses using different products on separate lines, as in <a href="/guides/paint-quantities-by-colour-and-finish/">grouping quantities by colour and finish</a>.</p>
<h2>Can the paint calculator check a vaulted-ceiling estimate?</h2>
<p>The room paint planner adds length × width for a ceiling and has no slope input. Calculate the vaulted area outside it, then use the paint coverage calculator to check whether your proposed containers cover that area. There is no custom-area field in the coverage tool: it converts your paint supply into practical project area.</p>
<p>For the 400 sq ft example, select these inputs:</p>
<ol>
<li>Paint format: Can or pail.</li>
<li>Result units: Square feet.</li>
<li>Number of containers: 3.</li>
<li>Volume per container: 1; Volume unit: US gallon.</li>
<li>Label coverage: 400 sq ft per US gallon, per coat.</li>
<li>Finish coats: 2; Contingency: 10%.</li>
</ol>
<p>The Practical project area result is about 545.5 sq ft, enough for the measured 400 sq ft. Two such cans give about 363.6 sq ft, which falls short at those inputs. Compare other available package sizes separately if you want to reduce leftover paint.</p>
<p>Selecting Square metres changes the result units, not the Label coverage field's units. A metric label of 10 m²/L converts to about 407.46 sq ft per US gallon: multiply by 10.7639 and 3.78541. Keep the metric purchasing worksheet alongside that conversion.</p>
<h2>What should you take to the paint counter?</h2>
<p>Keep a short shopping note: ceiling-plane measurements, total area, exact product, colour, finish, label coverage, coats, allowance and package size. For the symmetrical example, that note ends with 2.20 calculated US gallons and three one-US-gallon cans; for the metric example, 7.73 calculated litres and two 4 L cans.</p>
<p>Open the <a href="/calculators/paint-coverage/">paint coverage calculator</a>, enter your proposed containers and check that Practical project area meets or exceeds your measured sloped area.</p>
<p>Sources checked November 2026.</p>

